WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.940
JWT bearer tokens should be validated by maintained authentication components rather than custom parsing.

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.640
A JWT is a structured value, but reading its fields is not the same as trusting it.

3
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:23.340
Validation needs to confirm that the token came from the right authority, is intended for the right API, has not expired, and was signed in a way the application expects.

4
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.740
Issuer validation confirms who issued the token.

5
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.400
Audience validation confirms that the token was intended for this API or resource.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:42.380
These checks matter because a token created for one client, API, environment, or identity provider should not be accepted somewhere else merely because its contents are readable.

7
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.780
Lifetime validation confirms expiration behavior and token freshness according to policy.

8
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.080
Clock skew should be deliberate rather than accidental.

9
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:58.300
A small allowance may be operationally useful, but broad or unreviewed skew can make token lifetime less meaningful than the team intended.

10
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:04.820
Signature and signing key validation confirm that the token has not been altered and was signed by a trusted key.

11
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.960
Algorithm expectations should also be explicit.

12
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:13.420
The application should not accept unexpected signing behavior because a token header asked for it.

13
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.960
Trust should come from configuration and validated metadata, not from untrusted token content.

14
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.760
Claims should be interpreted only after validation succeeds.

15
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.420
Before validation, claim values are just data inside a presented token.

16
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:37.680
After validation, the application can map claims into identity and policy decisions according to issuer, audience, token type, and business context.

17
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.040
Authentication failure behavior should be predictable and safe.

18
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.780
Clients need consistent outcomes, and operators need protected diagnostic context.

19
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:56.240
User-facing or client-facing errors should not reveal unnecessary validation internals, while logs can record safe failure categories for troubleshooting.
